1. The HLD Problem
Imagine a real-time notification system: User opens the application → server needs to notify the user immediately when a new event arrives.
Examples:
	• Chat applications 
	• Stock price updates 
	• Order-status tracking 
	• Notifications 
	• Gaming 
	• Live dashboards 
	
The key HLD question is: How does the server deliver new data to the client without the client constantly refreshing?
Two common approaches are:

                 Real-Time Communication
                              |
             +-----------+-----------+
             |                                 |
        Long Polling             WebSocket
             |                                |
     HTTP connection          Persistent TCP
     repeatedly requested     bidirectional channel

2. Long Polling
Normal polling looks like:

Client                         Server
  |                                                 |
  |---- GET /notifications ----->|
  |<------- empty --------------    |
  |                                                |
  |---- GET /notifications ----->|
  |<------- empty --------------    |
  |                                                 |
  |---- GET /notifications ----->|
  |<----- notification ----------   |
The client keeps asking:
	"Anything new?"
	
Long Polling improves this
Instead of immediately responding, the server holds the HTTP request until data becomes available.

Client                                     Server
  |                                                |
  |---- GET /notifications ----->|
  |                                                |
  |         WAIT...                          |
  |                                                |
  |         WAIT...                          |
  |                                                |
  |<----- notification ----------   |
  |                                                 |
  |---- GET /notifications ----->|
  |                                                 |
So the client makes another request after receiving a response.

3. WebSockets
WebSocket works differently.
First, the client establishes a connection:

Client                         Server
  |                                                     |
  |---- HTTP Upgrade ----------->   |
  |<--- 101 Switching Protocols - |
  |                                                      |
  |====== WebSocket OPEN ========|
Now the connection stays open.
Either side can send messages.

Client                                     Server
  |                                                |
  |<------- notification --------   |
  |                                                |
  |-------- message ------------> |
  |                                                |
  |<------- notification --------   |
  |                                                |
  |<------- notification --------   |
  |                                                |
  |-------- message ------------> |
That's the fundamental difference.

4. HLD Comparison
	Long Polling	WebSocket
Protocol	HTTP	WebSocket
Connection	Repeated HTTP requests	Persistent
Communication	Mostly client → server requests	Bidirectional
Server push	Yes, indirectly	Yes, natively
Latency	Low	Very low
HTTP overhead	Higher	Lower after handshake
Scalability	Easier	More challenging
Load balancer	Simpler	Needs WebSocket support
Stateful connection	No	Yes
Best for	Occasional updates	Continuous real-time
Complexity	Low	Medium/High

